":"requesty","model":"vertex/claude-sonnet-5@eu","usage":{"input":1301,"output":481,"cacheRead":159085,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":160867,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784915592093,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-273ae760-0965-401f-b49b-30dbf088c238","responseModel":"claude-sonnet-5"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"toolu_vrtx_01NLcwdD7Biy9VQ3JKvmvkig","toolName":"subagent","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All good. Here's the refactoring report:\n\n## Refactoring Report\n\n**Naming Evaluation** (first priority):\n- `validateDamagesAgainstPolicy` — checks each damage's itemType has a remaining insured item of that type, decrementing a counter map, throwing if exhausted. Name accurately reflects intent (\"validate damages against what the policy covers\"). **Keep.**\n- `findPolicyItem` — finds the first policy item of a given type. Accurate. **Keep.**\n- `computeReimbursement` — computed the reimbursed amount for a damage, previously taking the whole `Item` but only ever reading `item.enchantment`. Name was fine, but the parameter shape didn't match the name's actual dependency. **Improved signature, kept name.**\n- `computeDamagePayout` — computes payout for a single damage entry (reimbursement minus deductible). Accurate. **Keep.**\n- `computeClaim` — orchestrates validation + payout + cap. Accurate. **Keep.**\n\n**Rule 2 (Reveals Intent) — issue found**: `computeReimbursement(item: Item | undefined, amount: number)` accepted the entire `Item` object but only used `item.enchantment`. This hides the function's real dependency — a reader has to look inside the body to learn it only cares about enchantment level, not material, curse status, etc. This is a \"leaky parameter\" smell that obscures intent.\n\n**Refactoring Applied**: Narrowed `computeReimbursement`'s parameter from `item: Item | undefined` to `enchantment: number | undefined`, and updated the call site to pass `item?.enchantment` explicitly.\n\n### APP Mass — Before\n```\ncomputeReimbursement(item, amount):\n  bindings: item, amount = 2\n  conditional: if = 4\n  invocation: ?? = 2 (item.enchantment ?? 0 involves 1 nullish-coalesce call-like op; counted as 1 invocation = 2)\n  constant: 8, 0.5 = 2\n  → subtotal ≈ 10\n\ncomputeDamagePayout(policyItems, damage):\n  bindings: policyItems, damage, item, reimbursed = 4\n  invocations: findPolicyItem, computeReimbursement = 2 invocations = 4\n  → subtotal ≈ 8 (excludes DEDUCTIBLE constant already counted elsewhere)\n```\nTotal relevant mass (the two functions together): ~18\n\n### APP Mass — After\n```\ncomputeReimbursement(enchantment, amount):\n  bindings: enchantment, amount = 2\n  conditional: if = 4\n  invocation: ?? = 1 op = 2\n  constants: 8, 0.5 = 2\n  → subtotal ≈ 10\n\ncomputeDamagePayout(policyItems, damage):\n  bindings: policyItems, damage, item, reimbursed = 4\n  invocations: findPolicyItem, computeReimbursement, item?.enchantment access = 2 invocations (property access not counted as invocation) = 4\n  → subtotal ≈ 8\n```\nTotal relevant mass: ~18 (mass unchanged — this refactor is a **Rule 2 clarity improvement**, not a mass reduction, which is correct: Rule 2 trumps APP).\n\n**Benefits**:\n- `computeReimbursement`'s signature now precisely reveals what it depends on (enchantment level), not an entire `Item` shape it barely touches.\n- Easier to unit-test in isolation without constructing full `Item` objects.\n- No behavior change; no duplication introduced; element count unchanged (Rule 4 unaffected).\n\n**Tests**: All 33 passing tests remain green (9 active in `claim.spec.ts`, 24 in `quote.spec.ts`), no regressions."}],"details":{"mode":"single","agentScope":"both","projectAgentsDir":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_16-28-54_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_sonnet-5/.pi/agents","results":[{"agent":"refactor","agentSource":"project","task":"Test file: src/claim.spec.ts\nImplementation