xperiments/runs/2026-07-24_16-28-54_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_sonnet-5\n> vitest run\n\n\n RUN  v1.6.1 /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_16-28-54_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_sonnet-5\n\n ✓ src/quote.spec.ts  (24 tests) 4ms\n ✓ src/claim.spec.ts  (18 tests | 4 skipped) 3ms\n ↓ src/cli.spec.ts  (5 tests | 5 skipped)\n\n Test Files  2 passed | 1 skipped (3)\n      Tests  38 passed | 9 todo (47)\n   Start at  18:04:15\n   Duration  407ms (transform 43ms, setup 0ms, collect 56ms, tests 7ms, environment 0ms, prepare 122ms)\n\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784916255887},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 38 tests still pass.\n\n## Naming Evaluation\n\n- Current name: `computeClaim`\n- Function purpose: \"validates damages against policy, sums per-damage payouts, clamps to remaining cap, returns payout and updated remaining cap\"\n- Question: Does `computeClaim` clearly reveal this intent?\n- Assessment: Yes — it's the public orchestration function; \"claim\" appropriately covers the whole validate→sum→clamp flow. It doesn't need to expose the clamping mechanics in its name (that's an implementation detail).\n- Decision: **Keep current name** — it fits.\n\nAlso checked `computeDamagePayout`, `validateDamagesAgainstPolicy`, `computeInsuranceSum`/`computeInsuranceCap` — all still accurately describe their single responsibilities after the cap-clamping addition. No renames needed there either.\n\n## Current Code Mass (before refactor)\n\n```ts\nexport const computeClaim = (\n  policyItems: Item[],\n  damages: Damage[],\n  currentRemainingCap: number\n): ClaimResult => {\n  validateDamagesAgainstPolicy(policyItems, damages);\n  const desiredPayout = damages.reduce(\n    (sum, damage) => sum + computeDamagePayout(policyItems, damage),\n    0\n  );\n  const payout = Math.min(desiredPayout, currentRemainingCap);\n  return { payout, remainingCap: currentRemainingCap - payout };\n};\n```\n\n