ing tests: 18 (all of claim.spec.ts; 42 total across project)\nRecent Green phase: validateDamagesAgainstPolicy now also throws if any damage.amount < 0, checked before the item-coverage check in the same per-damage loop.\n\nThis completes all planned claim.spec.ts tests (18/18 passing). Please do a thorough refactor pass over the whole file now that all tests are passing, since this is a natural milestone.\n\nRefactor the implementation while keeping all tests green.\n\nRun autonomously, return when done."}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"vertex/claude-sonnet-5@eu","usage":{"input":1050,"output":461,"cacheRead":192518,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":194029,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784916455138,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-c26db690-f3ba-4cf5-bf19-d931992ba348","responseModel":"claude-sonnet-5"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"toolu_vrtx_01JmwSy4LMtMXFwc6MwdQt5u","toolName":"subagent","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Refactoring Report\n\n### Step 1: Naming Evaluation (FIRST PRIORITY)\n\nWent through every function name in `claim.ts` against what it actually does now that all 18 tests pass:\n\n- `roundDownToNearestGold`, `findPolicyItem`, `computeInsuranceSum`, `computeInsuranceCap`, `computeReimbursement`, `computeDamagePayout`, `computeDesiredPayout`, `computeClaim` — all still precisely match their behavior. No renames needed.\n- `validateDamagesAgainstPolicy` — **this one no longer fit**. After the last Green phase, this single function was doing two unrelated checks in one loop:\n  1. \"Is `damage.amount` negative?\" — a standalone data-validity check, independent of the policy.\n  2. \"Does the policy have a remaining item of this type?\" — the actual \"against policy\" check the name promises.\n\n  The name `validateDamagesAgainstPolicy` accurately describes only check #2. A reader seeing that name would not expect it to also reject negative amounts — that's not a policy-coverage concern at all (it would be equally invalid against *any* policy). This is exactly the \"has the function's purpose become murkier through the latest test\" signal Rule 2 asks about.\n\n**Decision**: Split the negative-amount check into its own function, `validateDamageAmountsAreNonNegative`, and let `validateDamagesAgainstPolicy` go back to meaning exactly what its name says.\n\n### Step 2: Initial APP Mass (the merged loop before refactor)\n\n```js\nconst validateDamagesAgainstPolicy = (policyItems, damages) => {\n  const remainingByType = new Map();               // binding:1\n  for (const item of policyItems) {                 // loop:5, binding:1\n    remainingByType.set(item.type, (remainingByType.get(item.type) ?? 0) + 1); // invocation x3:6, const:1\n  }\n  for (const damage of damages) {                    // loop:5, binding:1\n    if (damage.amount < 0) {                          // conditional:4, const:1\n      throw new Error(...);                           // invocation:2\n    }\n    const remaining = remainingByType.get(damage.itemType) ?? 0;  // binding:1, invocation:2, const:1\n    if (remaining <= 0) {                              // conditional:4, const:1\n      throw new Error(...);                            // invocation:2\n    }\n    remainingByType.set(damage.itemType, remaining - 1); // invocation:2, const:1\n  }\n};\n```\nTotal ≈ bindings 4 + constants 4 + invocations 15 + conditionals 8 + loops 10 = **41**\n\n### Step 3: Simple Design Rules Applied\n\n- **Rule 1 (Tests Pass)**: All 18 `claim.spec.ts` tests + full 42-test suite green before and after.\n- **Rule 2 (Reveals Intent)** — primary driver here: extracting the negative-amount check restores accuracy to `validateDamagesAgainstPolicy`'s name and gives the new check its own honest name, `validateDamageAmountsAreNonNegative`.\n- **Rule 3 (No Duplication)**: No duplication introduced or found elsewhere in the file.\n- **Rule 4 (Fewest Elements)**: One new top-level function added — justified because it removes a conflation, not because it adds abstraction for its own sake.\n\n### Step 5: Refactored Mass\n\n```js\nconst validateDamageAmountsAreNonNegative = (damages) => {\n  for (const damage of damages) {         // loop:5, binding:1\n    if (damage.amount < 0) {               // conditional:4, const:1\n      throw new Error(...);                // invocation:2\n    }\n  }\n};\n// = 13\n\nconst validateDamagesAgainstPolicy = (policyItems, damages) => {\n  const remainingByType = new Map();       // binding:1\n  for (const item of policyItems) {         // loop:5, binding:1\n    remainingByType.set(...);               // invocation x3:6, const:1\n  }\n  for (const damage of damages) {            // loop:5, binding:1\n    const remaining = ... ?? 0;               // binding:1, invocation:2, const:1\n    if (remaining <= 0) {                      // conditional:4, const:1\n      throw new Error(...);                    // invocation:2\n    }\n    remainingByType.set(...);                   // invocation:2, const:1\n  }\n};\n// = 34\n```\nCombined new total: 13 + 34 = **47** (call site adds one more invocation: `validateDamageAmountsAreNonNegative(damages)` = +2, so **49** vs original 41 + call-site 2 = 43).\n\n**Mass Change: ~43 → ~49 (delta +6)**\n\n### Step 6: Documented Decision\n\n**Refactoring Applied**: Split the negative-amount check out of `validateDamagesAgainstPolicy` into `validateDamageAmountsAreNonNegative`, called before the policy-coverage check in `computeClaim`.\n\nThis is a case where **Rule 2 (clarity) correctly overrides APP mass** (per the explicit guideline: \"Rule 2 trumps APP\"). Mass went up slightly (extra function boundary, extra loop, extra invocation) but:\n- `validateDamagesAgainstPolicy` now does exactly one thing implied by its name: checks damages against the policy's item coverage.\n- The negative-amount rule is now a self-explanatory, independently-testable unit with its own accurate name.\n- Two single-purpose loops replace one loop silently doing two unrelated jobs, which is easier to read, reason about, and modify without collateral risk (e.g., changing the negative-amount rule can no longer accidentally perturb the item-consumption bookkeeping in the same loop).\n\nI left everything else in the file untouched: naming, structure, and comments in the rest of `claim.ts` already accurately reflect current behavior (verified explicitly against `computeInsuranceCap`, `computeDamagePayout`, `computeDesiredPayout`, `computeClaim`), there's no duplication to extract, and no unnecessary elements to remove.\n\n---\n\n## Refactoring Complete\n\n**Refactoring**: Extracted `validateDamageAmountsAreNonNegative` from `validateDamagesAgainstPolicy` to restore accurate naming (Rule 2) after the negative-amount check was folded into the wrong function during the last Green phase.\n**Mass Change**: ~43 → ~49 (+6) — accepted because clarity (Rule 2) outranks APP mass per the stated priority order.\n**Tests**: All 42 tests passing (18/18 in `claim.spec.ts`, 24/24 in `quote.spec.ts`, 5 todo in `cli.spec.ts` unaffected)."}],"details":{"mode":"single","agentScope":"both","projectAgentsDir":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_16-28-54_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_sonnet-5/.pi/agents","results":[{"agent":"refactor","agentSource":"project","task":"Test file: src/claim.spec.ts\nImplementation file: src/claim.ts\nPassing tests: 18 (all of claim.spec.ts; 42 total across project)\nRecent Green phase: validateDamagesAgainstPolicy now also throws if any damage.amount < 0, checked before the item-coverage check in the same per-damage loop.\n\nThis completes all planned claim.spec.ts tests (18/18 passing). Please do a thorough refactor pass over the whole file now that all tests are passing, since this is a natural milestone.\n\nRefactor the implementation while keeping all tests green.\n\nRun autonomously, return when done.","exitCode":0,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Task: Test file: src/claim.spec.ts\nImplementation file: src/claim.ts\nPassing tests: 18 (all of claim.spec.ts; 42 total across project)\nRecent Green phase: validateDamagesAgainstPolicy now also throws if any damage.amount < 0, checked before the item-coverage check in the same per-damage loop.\n\nThis completes all planned claim.spec.ts tests (18/18 passing). Please do a thorough refactor pass over the whole file now that all tests are passing, since this is a natural milestone.\n\nRefactor the implementation while keeping all tests green.\n\nRun autonomously, return when done."}],"timestamp":1784916460840},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_vrtx_01TMqrcYJXzBmWy4VF1giq1r","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"src/claim.ts"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_vrtx_013CcFsSKVM2ivueSZt3nVuS","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"src/claim.spec.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"vertex/claude-sonnet-5@eu","usage":{"input":283,"output":114,"cacheRead":9756,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":10153,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784916460860,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-a30e9301-7131-496d-bfbe-fd38cad7cb10","responseModel":"claude-sonnet-5"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"toolu_vrtx_01TMqrcYJXzBmWy4VF1giq1r","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"import type { Item, Damage, ClaimResult } from \"./types.js\";\nimport { correctFloatingPointError } from \"./rounding.js\";\n\nconst DEDUCTIBLE = 100;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_REIMBURSEMENT_RATE = 0.5;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 8;\n\n// Rounds down to the nearest whole gold piece, in the MHPCO's favor for\n// payouts (the opposite direction from premium rounding in quote.ts).\nconst roundDownToNearestGold = (amount: number): number =>\n  Math.floor(correctFloatingPointError(amount));\n\nconst findPolicyItem = (policyItems: Item[], itemType: string): Item | undefined =>\n  policyItems.find((item) => item.type === itemType);\n\nconst INSURANCE_VALUE_BY_ITEM_TYPE: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 1000,\n  amulet: 600,\n  staff: 800,\n  potion: 400,\n  rune: 250,\n  moonstone: 250,\n};\n\nconst CAP_MULTIPLIER = 2;\n\n// Named to match the domain language used in the policy spec (\"insurance\n// sum\" vs. \"cap\"), mirroring how quote.ts separates sumBasePremiums from\n// the surcharges/discounts applied on top of it.\nconst computeInsuranceSum = (policyItems: Item[]): number =>\n  policyItems.reduce((sum, item) => sum + INSURANCE_VALUE_BY_ITEM_TYPE[item.type], 0);\n\nexport const computeInsuranceCap = (policyItems: Item[]): number =>\n  computeInsuranceSum(policyItems) * CAP_MULTIPLIER;\n\nconst validateDamagesAgainstPolicy = (policyItems: Item[], damages: Damage[]): void => {\n  const remainingByType = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const item of policyItems) {\n    remainingByType.set(item.type, (remainingByType.get(item.type) ?? 0) + 1);\n  }\n  for (const damage of damages) {\n    if (damage.amount < 0) {\n      throw new Error(`Damage amount cannot be negative: ${damage.amount}`);\n    }\n    const remaining = remainingByType.get(damage.itemType) ?? 0;\n    if (remaining <= 0) {\n      throw new Error(`Damage references item type \"${damage.itemType}\" not covered by the policy`);\n    }\n    remainingByType.set(damage.itemType, remaining - 1);\n  }\n};\n\nconst computeReimbursement = (enchantment: number | undefined, amount: number): number => {\n  if ((enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD) {\n    return amount * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_REIMBURSEMENT_RATE;\n  }\n  return amount;\n};\n\nconst computeDamagePayout = (policyItems: Item[], damage: Damage): number => {\n  const item = findPolicyItem(policyItems, damage.itemType);\n  const reimbursed = computeReimbursement(item?.enchantment, damage.amount);\n  return reimbursed - DEDUCTIBLE;\n};\n\n// Named and extracted to mirror computeInsuranceSum's shape (reduce over\n// policy items to a single total), keeping computeClaim itself a plain\n// sequence of named steps rather than mixing an inline reduce with the\n// cap-clamping logic.\nconst computeDesiredPayout = (policyItems: Item[], damages: Damage[]): number =>\n  damages.reduce((sum, damage) => sum + computeDamagePayout(policyItems, damage), 0);\n\nexport const computeClaim = (\n  policyItems: Item[],\n  damages: Damage[],\n  currentRemainingCap: number\n): ClaimResult => {\n  validateDamagesAgainstPolicy(policyItems, damages);\n  const desiredPayout = computeDesiredPayout(policyItems, damages);\n  const payout = roundDownToNearestGold(Math.min(desiredPayout, currentRemainingCap));\n  return { payout, remainingCap: currentRemainingCap - payout };\n};\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784916462305},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"toolu_vrtx_013CcFsSKVM2ivueSZt3nVuS","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"import { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\nimport { computeClaim, computeInsuranceCap } from \"./claim.js\";\n\ndescribe(\"Claim - Payout Calculation\", () => {\n  // --- Standard reimbursement (no special clauses) ---\n  it(\n    \"regular sword (steel, enchantment 3), damage 500 G -> payout 400 G \" +\n      \"(full reimbursement minus 100 G deductible; no special clause applies)\",\n    () => {\n      const result = computeClaim(\n        [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3 }],\n        [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 500 }],\n        2000\n      );\n      expect(result.payout).toBe(400);\n    }\n  );\n  it(\n    \"damage to a rune (insurance value 250 G), damage 200 G -> payout 100 G \" +\n      \"(full reimbursement minus 100 G deductible; runes have no enchantment level or material)\",\n    () => {\n      const result = computeClaim([{ type: \"rune\" }], [{ itemType: \"rune\", amount: 200 }], 500);\n      expect(result.payout).toBe(100);\n    }\n  );\n\n  // --- Deductible per damage event ---\n  it(\n    \"dragon attack damages an insured sword (500 G) and an insured amulet (300 G) -> payout 600 G \" +\n      \"(100 G deductible applies once per damaged item)\",\n    () => {\n      const result = computeClaim(\n        [{ type: \"sword\" }, { type: \"amulet\" }],\n        [\n          { itemType: \"sword\", amount: 500 },\n          { itemType: \"amulet\", amount: 300 },\n        ],\n        3200\n      );\n      expect(result.payout).toBe(600);\n    }\n  );\n\n  // --- High enchantment clause (>= 8) ---\n  it(\n    \"steel sword, enchantment 9, damage 1000 G -> payout 400 G \" +\n      \"(only high-enchantment clause applies: 50% first, then deductible: 500 - 100)\",\n    () => {\n      const result = computeClaim(\n        [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 9 }],\n        [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 1000 }],\n        2000\n      );\n      expect(result.payout).toBe(400);\n    }\n  );\n\n  // --- Dragon material clause ---\n  it(\n    \"dragon-material sword, enchantment 5, damage 800 G -> payout 700 G \" +\n      \"(only dragon-material clause applies: full reimbursement, then deductible: 800 - 100)\",\n    () => {\n      const result = computeClaim(\n        [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"dragon\", enchantment: 5 }],\n        [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 800 }],\n        2000\n      );\n      expect(result.payout).toBe(700);\n    }\n  );\n\n  // --- Both clauses together (dragon-material wins) ---\n  it(\n    \"dragon-material sword, enchantment 9, damage 1000 G -> payout 400 G \" +\n      \"(both clauses apply; 50% rule wins, then deductible: 500 - 100)\",\n    () => {\n      const result = computeClaim(\n        [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"dragon\", enchantment: 9 }],\n        [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 1000 }],\n        2000\n      );\n      expect(result.payout).toBe(400);\n    }\n  );\n  it(\n    \"dragon-material sword with exactly enchantment 8, damage 1000 G -> payout 400 G \" +\n      \"(high-enchantment clause applies at exact threshold, then deductible)\",\n    () => {\n      const result = computeClaim(\n        [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"dragon\", enchantment: 8 }],\n        [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 1000 }],\n        2000\n      );\n      expect(result.payout).toBe(400);\n    }\n  );\n\n  // --- Multiple items of the same type ---\n  it(\n    \"policy covers two swords, dragon attack damages both (two separate {itemType: 'sword'} damage entries) \" +\n      \"-> each entry treated as a separate damage with its own deductible\",\n    () => {\n      const result = computeClaim(\n        [{ type: \"sword\" }, { type: \"sword\" }],\n        [\n          { itemType: \"sword\", amount: 500 },\n          { itemType: \"sword\", amount: 300 },\n        ],\n        4000\n      );\n      // (500-100) + (300-100) = 400 + 200 = 600\n      expect(result.payout).toBe(600);\n    }\n  );\n  it(\n    \"damages array contains more entries of a given type than the policy covers \" +\n      \"(e.g. two sword damages but only one sword insured) -> throws an error\",\n    () => {\n      expect(() =>\n        computeClaim(\n          [{ type: \"sword\" }],\n          [\n            { itemType: \"sword\", amount: 500 },\n            { itemType: \"sword\", amount: 300 },\n          ],\n          2000\n        )\n      ).toThrow();\n    }\n  );\n\n  // --- Cap exhaustion ---\n  it(\"policy covers a sword and an amulet -> insurance sum 1600 G (1000+600), cap 3200 G\", () => {\n    const cap = computeInsuranceCap([{ type: \"sword\" }, { type: \"amulet\" }]);\n    expect(cap).toBe(3200);\n  });\n  it(\n    \"cursed sword (insurance value 1000 G, premium with modifiers 165 G) -> cap 2000 G \" +\n      \"(based on unmodified insurance value; premium modifiers do not raise the cap)\",\n    () => {\n      const cap = computeInsuranceCap([{ type: \"sword\", cursed: true }]);\n      expect(cap).toBe(2000);\n    }\n  );\n  it(\n    \"policy covers a sword and 3 runes (a block) -> insurance sum 1750 G (1000 + 3x250); \" +\n      \"block discount affects premium only, not insurance sum\",\n    () => {\n      const cap = computeInsuranceCap([\n        { type: \"sword\" },\n        { type: \"rune\" },\n        { type: \"rune\" },\n        { type: \"rune\" },\n      ]);\n      // insurance sum 1750, cap 3500\n      expect(cap).toBe(3500);\n    }\n  );\n  it(\n    \"sword insured (insurance sum 1000 G, cap 2000 G); two successive claims of 1500 G each: \" +\n      \"first claim -> payout 1400 G, cap remaining 600 G\",\n    () => {\n      const result = computeClaim([{ type: \"sword\" }], [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 1500 }], 2000);\n      // desired payout: 1500 - 100 = 1400; within cap 2000, so full amount applies\n      expect(result.payout).toBe(1400);\n      expect(result.remainingCap).toBe(600);\n    }\n  );\n  it(\n    \"sword insured (insurance sum 1000 G, cap 2000 G); two successive claims of 1500 G each: \" +\n      \"second claim -> payout 600 G, cap remaining 0 G (desired 1400 G reduced to remaining cap)\",\n    () => {\n      // After the first claim, remaining cap is 600 G. Desired payout is again 1400 G,\n      // but must be clamped to the 600 G remaining on the cap.\n      const result = computeClaim([{ type: \"sword\" }], [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 1500 }], 600);\n      expect(result.payout).toBe(600);\n      expect(result.remainingCap).toBe(0);\n    }\n  );\n\n  // --- Rounding in the MHPCO's favor ---\n  it(\"payout calculation yielding 350.5 G rounds down to 350 G\", () => {\n    const result = computeClaim(\n      [{ type: \"sword\", enchantment: 9 }],\n      [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 901 }],\n      2000\n    );\n    // reimbursed = 901 * 0.5 = 450.5, - 100 deductible = 350.5, rounds down to 350\n    expect(result.payout).toBe(350);\n  });\n\n  // --- Error cases ---\n  it(\n    \"claim references a damage entry whose item is not part of the policy \" +\n      \"(e.g. an amulet damaged when only a sword is insured) -> throws an error\",\n    () => {\n      expect(() =>\n        computeClaim([{ type: \"sword\" }], [{ itemType: \"amulet\", amount: 300 }], 2000)\n      ).toThrow();\n    }\n  );\n  it(\"claim references a damage entry with an unknown item type -> throws an error\", () => {\n    expect(() =>\n      computeClaim([{ type: \"sword\" }], [{ itemType: \"broomstick\", amount: 300 }], 2000)\n    ).toThrow();\n  });\n  it(\"claim contains a damage entry with amount: -200 -> throws an error\", () => {\n    expect(() =>\n      computeClaim([{ type: \"sword\" }], [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: -200 }], 2000)\n    ).toThrow();\n  });\n});\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784916462305},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_vrtx_01H7eBkhmHigMWDKwipsqeRh","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"src/types.ts"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_vrtx_01XKrE5PKG4dEdSXSu1LhfcT","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"src/rounding.ts"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_vrtx_0184CaWEzym9AHs5wirkZReG","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_16-28-54_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_sonnet-5 && ls src/ && cat package.json | head -30"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"vertex/claude-sonnet-5@eu","usage":{"input":4890,"output":460,"cacheRead":9756,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":15106,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784916462305,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-4d4451b9-6e5b-4658-baee-bfd78c8027ce","responseModel":"claude-sonnet-5"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"toolu_vrtx_01H7eBkhmHigMWDKwipsqeRh","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"export interface Item {\n  type: string;\n  material?: string;\n  enchantment?: number;\n  cursed?: boolean;\n}\n\nexport interface QuoteCustomer {\n  yearsWithMHPCO: number;\n  isFollowUpContract: boolean;\n}\n\nexport interface Damage {\n