ap iteration, mixing an untyped `unknown[]` cast (`step.items as never`) with an inline object construction. This obscured that the customer is a single, stable value across all steps, and the `as never` cast hid a type mismatch rather than expressing intent. Extracted `customer` as a named, typed (`QuoteCustomer`) binding computed once, and introduced a `QuoteStep` interface so `step.items` is properly typed as `Item[]` without unsafe casting. Added a comment explaining why `isFollowUpContract` is hardcoded to `false` — this was implicit and easy to miss before.\n- **Rule 3 (No Duplication)**: No duplication found.\n- **Rule 4 (Fewest Elements)**: No unnecessary elements introduced; the new interfaces (`QuoteStep`) and typed `customer` binding are minimal additions.\n\n## Refactored Code Mass (after)\n\n```typescript\nconst customer: QuoteCustomer = {\n  yearsWithMHPCO: input.customer.yearsWithMHPCO,\n  isFollowUpContract: false,\n};\nconst results = input.steps.map((step: QuoteStep) => ({\n  premium: computePremium(customer, step.items),\n}));\nprocess.stdout.write(JSON.stringify({ results }));\n```\n\n- Bindings: `customer`, `input`, `results`, `step` = 4\n- Constants: `false` = 1\n- Invocations: `JSON.parse`, `readStdin`, `map`, `computePremium`, `JSON.stringify`, `process.stdout.write` = 6 × 2 = 12\n- Conditionals: 0\n- Loops: `map` = 1 × 5 = 5\n- Assignments: 0 (object literal init isn't a mutation)\n\n**Total Mass: 4 + 1 + 12 + 5 = 22**\n\n**Mass Change: 21 → 22 (delta +1)**\n\n## Refactoring Applied\n\n- Extracted the previously-inline, hastily-typed customer object into a named `customer: QuoteCustomer` binding built once outside the map — removing the unsafe `step.items as never` cast by properly typing `step` via a new `QuoteStep` interface.\n- Documented the hardcoded `isFollowUpContract: false` decision inline, since it's a real business rule gap (no step signals follow-up contracts yet) that was previously invisible.\n\nMass increased by 1 (one extra b