[{"type":"text","text":"import { computePremium } from \"./quote.js\";\nimport { computeClaim, computeInsuranceCap } from \"./claim.js\";\nimport type { Item, QuoteCustomer, Damage, ClaimResult } from \"./types.js\";\n\ninterface QuoteStep {\n  op: \"quote\";\n  items: Item[];\n}\n\ninterface ClaimStep {\n  op: \"claim\";\n  policy: number;\n  incident: { cause: string; damages: Damage[] };\n}\n\ntype Step = QuoteStep | ClaimStep;\n\ninterface PolicyRecord {\n  items: Item[];\n  remainingCap: number;\n}\n\ninterface QuoteResult {\n  premium: number;\n}\n\ntype StepResult = QuoteResult | ClaimResult;\n\nconst readStdin = async (): Promise<string> => {\n  const chunks: Buffer[] = [];\n  for await (const chunk of process.stdin) {\n    chunks.push(chunk);\n  }\n  return Buffer.concat(chunks).toString(\"utf-8\");\n};\n\nconst processStep = (\n  step: Step,\n  customer: QuoteCustomer,\n  policiesByStepIndex: Map<number, PolicyRecord>,\n  stepIndex: number\n): StepResult => {\n  if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n    const premium = computePremium(customer, step.items);\n    policiesByStepIndex.set(stepIndex, { items: step.items, remainingCap: computeInsuranceCap(step.items) });\n    return { premium };\n  }\n  const policy = policiesByStepIndex.get(step.policy)!;\n  const result = computeClaim(policy.items, step.incident.damages, policy.remainingCap);\n  policy.remainingCap = result.remainingCap;\n  return result;\n};\n\nconst main = async (): Promise<void> => {\n  const input = JSON.parse(await readStdin());\n  // isFollowUpContract is hardcoded to false: no step type yet signals a\n  // follow-up contract, so every quote is treated as a new one. The\n  // customer is built once, since it stays the same across every step.\n  const customer: QuoteCustomer = {\n    yearsWithMHPCO: input.customer.yearsWithMHPCO,\n    isFollowUpContract: false,\n  };\n  const policiesByStepIndex = new Map<number, PolicyRecord>();\n  const results = input.steps.map((step: Step, index: number) =>\n    processStep(step, customer, policiesByStepIndex, index)\n  );\n  process.stdout.write(JSON.stringify({ results }));\n};\n\nmain();\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784917331092},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Refactored Code Mass**:\nRuntime shape of `processStep` is unchanged (the added `QuoteResult`/`StepResult` types are compile-time only, erased at runtime — they carry no bindings, invocations, conditionals, loops, or assignments in the running program).\n\nTotal Mass: **27 → 27** (unchanged)\n\n**Refactoring Applied**:\n- **Naming**: Evaluated `processStep` and all other names — already accurately reveal intent. Kept as-is.\n- **Rule 2 (Reveals Intent)**: The return type `unknown` on `processStep` hid the fact that the function returns exactly one of two well-defined shapes (`{ premium: number }` or `ClaimResult`). Introduced `QuoteResult` and `type StepResult = QuoteResult | ClaimResult` and used `StepResult` as the explicit return type.\n- **Rule 3 (Duplication)**: None found — the quote/claim branches don't share extractable logic.\n- **Rule 4 (Fewest Elements)**: No unnecessary abstractions found elsewhere; `PolicyRecord` remains minimal and necessary.\n- **APP Mass**: 27 → 27 (no runtime cost — this is a pure type-level clarity improvement, consistent with Rule 2 trumping APP when there's nothing to trade off).\n\nBenefits:\n- Readers and future maintainers (and the type checker) now see explicitly what `processStep` can return, instead of an escape hatch (`unknown`) that would silently permit any misuse of the result at call sites.\n- `main`'s `results` array is now correctly typed as `StepResult[]` rather than `unknown[]`.\n\n## Refactoring Complete\n\n**Refactoring**: Replaced `unknown` return type on `processStep` with an explicit `StepResult = QuoteResult | ClaimResult` union type, making the dual-shape output self-documenting.\n**Mass Change**: 27 → 27 (type-only change, no runtime cost; clarity improvement doesn't requi